= Coding and comparison notes for qualitative review

Thirty short interviews were coded with one codebook in two editions. Two coders annotated overlapping passages, and disagreements between the coders were retained rather than resolved by discussion.

Claims are compared only across the same frozen snapshot. A frozen snapshot names the exact revisions it contains, so comparisons never mix revisions from different freezes.

Re-freezing after an edit produces a new snapshot and never rewrites an existing one. The earlier snapshot stays readable, and the new manifest lists its own members independently.

Supporting and challenging links are stored as separate attributed records. An author who recorded a link is not an author who endorses the claim, and the review notes keep those roles distinct.

Where the evidence conflicted, both links were kept and the conflict was reported in the review session. No ranking, scoring, or automatic selection was applied to the retained quotations at any point.

Analytical reports use the structured survey rows only. Presentation format is reported separately, so a change of layout cannot silently change an analytical value.

Coding disagreements were counted per passage rather than averaged per coder. Averaging would hide the passages that caused the most difficulty, and those passages are the most informative part of the review.

The second edition of the codebook replaced one broad code with two narrower codes. Passages coded under the earlier edition keep their original code and their original revision identifier.

Correcting the coordinator's job title did not move any quotation. Source corrections create new revisions, and existing citations continue to point at the bytes they were recorded against.

Each review session ends with a short written summary that names the snapshot used, the codes applied, and every conflict that remains visible in the trace.
